{"tool_name":"seqplots","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"seqplots","home":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/seqplots.html","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Tool for plotting NGS based experiments' signal tracks, e.g. reads coverage from ChIP-seq, RNA-seq and DNA accessibility assays like DNase-seq and MNase-seq, over user specified genomic features, e.g. promoters, gene bodies, etc. It can also calculate sequence motif density profiles from a reference genome.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:57:02Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:58:54Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.12.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:seqplots","credit":[{"email":"ps562@cam.ac.uk","name":"Przemyslaw Stempor","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/seqplots.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Annotation track","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3002"},"format":[{"term":"BED","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3003"},{"term":"GFF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2305"},{"term":"WIG","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3005"}]},{"data":{"term":"Feature table","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1270"},"format":[{"term":"BED","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3003"},{"term":"GFF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2305"},{"term":"WIG","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3005"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Sequence visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0564"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Heat map","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1636"},"format":[{"term":"PDF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3508"}]},{"data":{"term":"Plot","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2884"},"format":[{"term":"PDF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3508"}]}]}],"name":"seqplots","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.12688/wellcomeopenres.10004.1","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2016 Stempor P and Ahringer J.Experiments involving high-throughput sequencing are widely used for analyses of chromatin function and gene expression. Common examples are the use of chromatin immunoprecipitation for the analysis of chromatin modifications or factor binding, enzymatic digestions for chromatin structure assays, and RNA sequencing to assess gene expression changes after biological perturbations. To investigate the pattern and abundance of coverage signals across regions of interest, data are often visualized as profile plots of average signal or stacked rows of signal in the form of heatmaps. We found that available plotting software was either slow and laborious or difficult to use by investigators with little computational training, which inhibited wide data exploration. To address this need, we developed SeqPlots, a user-friendly exploratory data analysis (EDA) and visualization software for genomics. After choosing groups of signal and feature files and defining plotting parameters, users can generate profile plots of average signal or heatmaps clustered using different algorithms in a matter of seconds through the graphical user interface (GUI) controls. SeqPlots accepts all major genomic file formats as input and can also generate and plot user defined motif densities. Profile plots and heatmaps are highly configurable and batch operations can be used to generate a large number of plots at once. SeqPlots is available as a GUI application for Mac or Windows and Linux, or as an R/Bioconductor package. It can also be deployed on a server for remote and collaborative usage. The analysis features and ease of use of SeqPlots encourages wide data exploration, which should aid the discovery of novel genomic associations.","authors":[{"name":"Ahringer J."},{"name":"Stempor P."}],"citationCount":46,"date":"2016-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Wellcome Open Research","title":"SeqPlots - Interactive software for exploratory data analyses, pattern discovery and visualization in genomics"},"type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Data visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0092"},{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"seqplots","home":"https://bio.tools/seqplots","license":"GPL-3.0","version":"1.12.0","summary":"Tool for plotting NGS based experiments' signal tracks, e.g. reads coverage from ChIP-seq, RNA-seq and DNA accessibility assays like DNase-seq and MNase-seq, over user specified genomic features, e.g. promoters, gene bodies, etc. It can also calculate sequence motif density profiles from a reference genome.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:seqplots:1.12.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":95,"citations":[{"count":3,"year":"2026"},{"count":5,"year":"2017"},{"count":7,"year":"2020"},{"count":8,"year":"2019"},{"count":9,"year":"2018"},{"count":10,"year":"2024"},{"count":10,"year":"2025"},{"count":12,"year":"2022"},{"count":14,"year":"2021"},{"count":17,"year":"2023"}],"doi":"10.12688/wellcomeopenres.10004.1","pmcid":"PMC5133382","pmid":"27918597","ref_count":8,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"2010"},{"count":1,"year":"2011"},{"count":1,"year":"2015"},{"count":2,"year":"2013"},{"count":3,"year":"2014"}],"title":"SeqPlots - Interactive software for exploratory data analyses, pattern discovery and visualization in genomics.","year":2016}]}],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https":true,"license":